  "readmeFile": "docs\\README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "More precise results, no new features: the platform's machinery, script names built at run time and report\nSQL are no longer listed as references of a field they do not prove.\n\n- A real-time workflow or a business rule runs through registrations that Microsoft's platform makes for it.\n  Registrations that cover every column (the full images the platform registers for every real-time workflow)\n  showed every such workflow of the table as a reference of every field. They are now listed only with their\n  workflow, when the workflow itself uses the field. A registration that names the field still stands for its\n  process, also when the process cannot be identified, and custom plug-in steps are listed as before.\n- Nothing is deleted: these registrations are kept, counted (\"platform registrations that cover every\n  column\"), listed with *Show Microsoft / platform references* and in a full export, each with the reason.\n  Counts, the impact assessment and a standard export follow what is listed.\n- Exports state why a reference is not listed: `hiddenReason` in JSON (format version 8) and the column\n  `HiddenReason` in CSV and Excel, after the existing columns.\n- **JavaScript: a control or column name built at run time is no longer a reference of every field.** A call\n  such as `getControl(\"header_process_\" + name)` could address any column of the form, yet it was listed as a\n  possible (uncertain) reference of whichever field was analysed. Such a call is now listed only when a\n  written part of the name is part of the field's own name (`prefix + \"score\"`); otherwise the coverage\n  counts it (\"N client API call(s) in N script(s) build the name of a column or control at run time ...\").\n  Names the script itself fixes are followed further than before, through the properties of object literals\n  (`Contoso.Order.Fields.Score`, `this.Fields.Score`, `fields[\"Score\"]`) and the variables that alias them,\n  and are matched exactly: a reference when they name the field, nothing when they name another one. A\n  definition does not apply where a parameter of the same name hides it. Such a reference is never verified\n  by its name alone: a resolved name is at most HEURISTIC, a computed one UNKNOWN.\n- **Reports: SQL data sets are analysed instead of searched as text.** A report whose SQL reads the field\n  through the table's filtered view (`SELECT CAST(FilteredAccount.revenue AS float) AS Revenue FROM\n  FilteredAccount`, or `c.revenue` with `FROM FilteredAccount AS c`) was listed as an uncertain text match\n  (*Definition not interpreted*). It is now a verified READ, FILTER or SORT, element by element: aliases,\n  joins of several tables, sub-queries, CTEs, derived tables, bracketed and qualified names are resolved; an\n  element that reads the same name from some other view goes to the excluded list, never to the count; what\n  the query returns is traced into the report fields and expressions that use it. What the query cannot\n  settle stays uncertain and says why (several tables could have the column, a source that is not a table of\n  the organization, SQL built at run time, a statement other than SELECT). Comments, captions and names of\n  result columns are no longer reported. Stored procedures and shared data sets, whose query is not in the\n  report, are stated as not inspected. SQL is still never executed.\n- Known limitation: a report whose query is a report expression (`=\"SELECT ...\" & ...`) builds its SQL only\n  when the report runs. Its references stay uncertain (*Dynamic field or entity name*) and say so; they are\n  not evaluated.",
  "requireLicenseAcceptance": true,
  "summary": "Analyze field dependencies and references across Microsoft Dynamics 365 and Dataverse components.",
